{"subjectId":"python","id":"cli-argparse","title":"Command-Line Scripts & argparse","group":"Standard Library","order":53,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Turning a Python file into a proper command-line tool: a <code>main()</code> entry point, arguments parsed with <code>argparse</code>, clean exit codes and input from files or stdin.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>The script skeleton</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"wordcount — count words in text files.\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport sys\n\n\ndef parse_args(argv=None):\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Count words in text files.\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"files\", nargs=\"+\", help=\"files to read\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"-n\", \"--top\", type=int, default=5, help=\"how many words to show\")\n    return parser.parse_args(argv)\n\n\ndef main(argv=None) -&gt; int:\n    args = parse_args(argv)\n    ...                       # do the work\n    return 0                  # exit code: 0 = success\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hash\"></i>Shebang</h3><p><code>#!/usr/bin/env python3</code> + <code>chmod +x wordcount.py</code> lets you run <code>./wordcount.py</code> directly on macOS/Linux.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-test-pipe\"></i><code>main(argv=None)</code></h3><p>Taking <code>argv</code> as a parameter makes the CLI testable: <code>main([\"a.txt\", \"-n\", \"3\"])</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-logout\"></i>Exit codes</h3><p>Return 0 on success and non-zero on failure — shells, CI and <code>&amp;&amp;</code> chains depend on it.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i>Entry points</h3><p>Packaged tools declare <code>[project.scripts] wordcount = \"pkg.cli:main\"</code> in <code>pyproject.toml</code>.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>sys.argv — the raw arguments</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import sys\n\nsaved = sys.argv\nsys.argv = [\"greet.py\", \"Asha\", \"--loud\"]    # what the shell would pass for: python greet.py Asha --loud\nname = sys.argv[1]\nloud = \"--loud\" in sys.argv[2:]\nprint((\"HELLO \" if loud else \"hello \") + name)   # HELLO Asha\nsys.argv = saved</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>sys.argv</code> is fine for one or two arguments. As soon as you have options, defaults, types or <code>--help</code>, switch to <code>argparse</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>argparse — positional, optional, flags</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import argparse\n\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\"resize\")\nparser.add_argument(\"image\")                                      # positional, required\nparser.add_argument(\"-w\", \"--width\", type=int, required=True)     # option with a value\nparser.add_argument(\"--format\", choices=[\"png\", \"jpg\"], default=\"png\")\nparser.add_argument(\"-q\", \"--quiet\", action=\"store_true\")         # flag: True if present\nparser.add_argument(\"-v\", \"--verbose\", action=\"count\", default=0) # -vv → 2\nparser.add_argument(\"--tag\", action=\"append\", default=[])         # repeatable\nparser.add_argument(\"extra\", nargs=\"*\")                           # zero or more\n\nargs = parser.parse_args([\"cat.png\", \"-w\", \"300\", \"-vv\", \"--tag\", \"a\", \"--tag\", \"b\", \"x\", \"y\"])\nprint(args.image, args.width, args.format, args.quiet)   # cat.png 300 png False\nprint(args.verbose, args.tag, args.extra)                # 2 ['a', 'b'] ['x', 'y']\nprint(vars(args)[\"width\"] * 2)                           # 600</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>type=int</code> / <code>float</code> / <code>Path</code> / any callable</td><td>Convert (and validate) the string</td></tr><tr><td><code>default=…</code></td><td>Value when the option is absent</td></tr><tr><td><code>required=True</code></td><td>Make an option mandatory</td></tr><tr><td><code>choices=[…]</code></td><td>Restrict to allowed values</td></tr><tr><td><code>nargs=\"?\" / \"*\" / \"+\" / 2</code></td><td>Optional one / any number / at least one / exactly two</td></tr><tr><td><code>action=\"store_true\"</code></td><td>Boolean flag</td></tr><tr><td><code>action=\"count\"</code></td><td>Count repetitions (<code>-vvv</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>action=\"append\"</code></td><td>Collect repeated options into a list</td></tr><tr><td><code>dest=\"name\"</code>, <code>metavar=\"N\"</code></td><td>Attribute name / how the value is shown in help</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-help\"></i>Errors and --help for free</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import argparse, io, contextlib\n\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\"resize\")\nparser.add_argument(\"-w\", \"--width\", type=int, required=True)\n\nerr = io.StringIO()\ntry:\n    with contextlib.redirect_stderr(err):\n        parser.parse_args([\"-w\", \"wide\"])\nexcept SystemExit as e:\n    print(\"exit code\", e.code)                          # exit code 2\nprint(err.getvalue().strip().splitlines()[-1])\n# resize: error: argument -w/--width: invalid int value: 'wide'</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>exit code 2\nresize: error: argument -w/--width: invalid int value: 'wide'</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ python resize.py --help\nusage: resize [-h] -w WIDTH [--format {png,jpg}] [-q] [-v] image\n\npositional arguments:\n  image\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  -w, --width WIDTH\n  --format {png,jpg}\n  -q, --quiet\n  -v, --verbose</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-branch\"></i>Subcommands (git-style)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import argparse\n\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\"todo\")\nsub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", required=True)\n\nadd = sub.add_parser(\"add\", help=\"add a task\")\nadd.add_argument(\"text\")\nadd.add_argument(\"--priority\", type=int, default=3)\n\ndone = sub.add_parser(\"done\", help=\"complete a task\")\ndone.add_argument(\"id\", type=int)\n\nprint(vars(parser.parse_args([\"add\", \"buy milk\", \"--priority\", \"1\"])))\n# {'command': 'add', 'text': 'buy milk', 'priority': 1}\nprint(vars(parser.parse_args([\"done\", \"7\"])))    # {'command': 'done', 'id': 7}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{'command': 'add', 'text': 'buy milk', 'priority': 1}\n{'command': 'done', 'id': 7}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-import\"></i>Reading input: files or stdin</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import io, sys\n\ndef count_lines(stream) -&gt; int:\n    return sum(1 for line in stream if line.strip())\n\nsaved = sys.stdin\nsys.stdin = io.StringIO(\"alpha\\n\\nbeta\\ngamma\\n\")    # pretend: cat notes.txt | python count.py\nprint(count_lines(sys.stdin))                              # 3\nsys.stdin = saved</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ python count.py notes.txt            # read a file named on the command line\n$ cat notes.txt | python count.py      # or read from a pipe\n$ python count.py &lt; notes.txt          # or from a redirect\n$ python count.py notes.txt &amp;&amp; echo ok # exit code 0 lets the chain continue</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>fileinput.input()</code> iterates over the lines of every file named in <code>sys.argv[1:]</code>, or stdin if there are none — the classic Unix-filter behaviour in one line.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sparkles\"></i><code>click</code> / <code>typer</code></h3><p>Third-party libraries that build CLIs from decorators or type hints — nicer for big tools.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-palette\"></i><code>rich</code></h3><p>Colour, tables and progress bars in the terminal.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why use raise SystemExit(main()) instead of just calling main()?</summary><div><p>It turns <code>main</code>'s return value into the process exit code, so shells and CI can tell success (0) from failure. Keeping logic in <code>main(argv)</code> also makes it importable and testable.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does argparse report errors?</summary><div><p>It prints a usage line and an error message to stderr and exits with status 2 (by raising <code>SystemExit</code>). <code>--help</code> prints help and exits with 0.</p></div></details></section>"}